<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuélap</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuélap is a walled settlement of the Chachapoya culture on a ridge above the Utcubamba valley in Amazonas, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, built mainly from about the 6th century CE. Its massive limestone walls, up to about 20 metres high, enclose more than 400 round stone buildings. Often compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a>, it is the best-known monument of the Chachapoya, who were conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> in the late 15th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuelap.jpg" alt="Kuélap" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin St-Amant (S23678) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuelap_-_Ao%C3%BBt_2007_-_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kuélap, also written Cuélap, is a walled settlement in the mountains near the towns of María and Tingo, in the southern part of the region of Amazonas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. It was built by the Chachapoyas culture on a limestone ridge overlooking the Utcubamba Valley, at an elevation of 3,000 metres on the left bank of the Utcubamba River, in the district of Tingo, Luya Province. The area is covered with cloud forests of trees, orchids and epiphytes. The protected zone covers 218.33 hectares and a buffer zone 609.67 hectares, which together protect about twelve archaeological sites and the main site of Kuélap.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The abandoned settlement covers about six hectares, 584 metres from north to south and 110 metres at its widest. Its walls are 10 to 20 metres high, built of finely worked limestone blocks, some of which may weigh three tons. It has three entrances, two on the east and one on the west, and the main entrance is trapezoidal and narrows until it allows the passage of one person. All but five of its 421 structures are circular, and some have friezes of rhomboid and zigzag shapes.</p><p>An inverted-cone structure 5.5 metres high, known as El Tintero or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a>, had ceremonial remains and may have served as a solar observatory. Many stones carry designs in relief, and burials have been found in the walls and inside the circular structures.</p><p>The majority of the structures were built between 900 and 1100 CE, and the city may have had some 3,000 inhabitants. It was abandoned in 1570 because of the Spanish Conquest. The judge Juan Crisóstomo Nieto of Chachapoyas accidentally rediscovered it in 1843, and Antonio Raimondi surveyed it in 1870. Charles Wiener visited in 1881, and Louis Langlois described the main buildings in 1939. Ernst Middendorf, Adolf Bandelier, Henry and Paule Reichlen and Arturo Ruíz also studied the site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since 2013 Kuélap has suffered structural instabilities, which culminated in the collapse of a section of the perimeter wall in April 2022. The Ministry of Culture launched emergency conservation measures and research with Peruvian and foreign experts. In 2026 archaeologists uncovered a stone funerary structure holding the remains of five individuals, four adults and an infant, with ceremonial offerings in a Regional Inca style, dated to the Late Horizon period (c. 1470-1532 CE). Visitors now reach the site by a cable car that takes them to within 2 km of the fortress.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuélap” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their most famous site, <b>Kuélap</b>, is a walled platform up to 20 metres high containing hundreds of round buildings.</blockquote><a href="/state/chachapoya">Chachapoya</a></li></ul></section>
